<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">1973 oil crisis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1973">1973</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as first oil shock</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1973 oil crisis began in October 1973, when the Organization of Arab Petroleum Exporting Countries, led by Faisal of Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, imposed an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/us-oil-embargo-against-japan">oil embargo</a> on countries that had supported Israel in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yom-kippur-war">Yom Kippur War</a>, first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, the United Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. With monthly production cuts, the price of oil rose by nearly 300%, from US$3 to nearly US$12 a barrel, before the embargo was lifted in March 1974. The shock set off a worldwide inflationary spiral and the steepest economic contraction since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>, ending the long post-war prosperity of the West. A second oil shock followed in 1979.</p></div>
